;; Reviewed 2026-10-10 (written by Claude, from design section 16, the
;; VAX architecture's floating point and the guest runs; for the user's
;; review). Why this is right:
;; - The VAX has no infinity and no NaN: case 7, x := zero / zero: a
;;   division by 0.0 is a floating divide by zero fault on the VAX,
;;   whatever is divided, before ENTIER is reached. LLVM's is a NaN,
;;   which ENTIER traps.
;; - VMS's own message for the fault, then its traceback: the module's
;;   initializer, called from the program's start. tools/vax-suite masks
;;   the code addresses, which any change to the code moves.
;; - The program has written nothing before the fault, so VMS's message
;;   and traceback are in its standard output as well as its standard
;;   error: a guest test (2026-10-10) showed them in a SYS$OUTPUT file
;;   the program had not opened, and not in one it had written to first.
;; - For every build; expected-vax-entiertrap-OC-8.out overrides it
;;   under -OC.
%SYSTEM-F-FLTDIV_F, arithmetic fault, floating divide by zero at PC=xxxxxxxx, PSL=xxxxxxxx
%TRACE-F-TRACEBACK, symbolic stack dump follows
module name     routine name                     line       rel PC    abs PC

ENTIERTRAP      ENTIERTRAP_INIT                            xxxxxxxx  xxxxxxxx
ENTIERTRAP      ENTIERTRAP_MAIN                            xxxxxxxx  xxxxxxxx
